\paragraph{Main multi-objective comparison.}
Table~\ref{tab:multi-dataset-external} reports the primary comparison. Every entry is measured on
prompt-disjoint held-out prompts ($698$ on \textsc{UltraFeedback}, $400$ on the other two panels),
judged by \texttt{Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct} in both presentation orders, with two policy samples
against two reference samples per prompt and objective. The stated uncertainty is a prompt-level
bootstrap standard error of the reported statistic, resampling prompts once per replicate and
recomputing the minimum and the mean across objectives jointly; because the arms share prompts, the
paired differences quoted below are far better resolved than these marginal errors suggest.
Appendix~\ref{app:objectivewise} reports the per-objective rates behind each entry.

The comparison does not support a single ordering, and we do not claim one. No method attains the
highest worst-objective win rate on all three panels: Panacea leads it on \textsc{UltraFeedback}
and \textsc{TL;DR}, while the game-aggregation controls lead it on \textsc{SafeRLHF}. \NBPO{} is
second of eleven on \textsc{TL;DR}, sixth on \textsc{UltraFeedback}, and ninth on
\textsc{SafeRLHF}, where it is the panel's compression rather than its own collapse that puts it
there: apart from Uniform-INPO and the single-objective corners, every arm on that panel lands
within $0.085$ of every other.

What the measurements do support is the weaker property the bargaining axioms actually assert,
and Figure~\ref{fig:worst-vs-mean} shows it directly. Individual rationality on every coordinate --- staying above the reference on every objective of
every panel --- holds for four of the eleven methods: \NBPO{}, MOPO, Rewarded Soups and Panacea.
The other three reach it by construction rather than as a consequence: Rewarded Soups and Panacea
interpolate or condition on a fitted scalar reward per objective and their operating point is
chosen on validation data by the largest minimum utility, and MOPO designates a primary objective
and imposes hand-set lower bounds on the rest. \NBPO{} is the only one that neither fits a scalar
reward per objective nor is given a floor to respect; individual rationality falls out of the
bargaining solution, which is what the axioms predict. The seven methods that do not attain
it fail badly rather than marginally: Uniform-INPO reaches $0.540$ on \textsc{UltraFeedback} but
$0.228$ on \textsc{TL;DR}, MaxMin-RLHF's alternation ends its last round at $0.346$ on
\textsc{TL;DR}'s conciseness, and the utilitarian and absolute max-min controls fall to $0.415$ and
$0.365$ there. \NBPO{}'s worst and average objective rates also differ by the least across the
table, though only just: at most $0.030$ against MOPO's $0.033$, and MOPO's spread is small mainly
because it stays close to the reference. None of this is the stronger claim that Nash aggregation
maximises the minimum in finite samples, which our own matched ablation does not establish.

