\paragraph{General capability.}
Table~\ref{tab:general-benchmark-completion} asks what each method costs outside its own objective
panel. On the \texttt{Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct} backbone the pairwise columns separate the methods
little, but IFEval separates them sharply, and it separates them against the game-aggregation
controls rather than against \NBPO{}: the reference policy scores $0.762$, \NBPO{} $0.756$, and
every control that shares its response pool and budget falls to between $0.617$ and $0.645$. Of the
fifteen trained arms \NBPO{} gives up the least instruction-following capability, $0.006$, and MOPO
is next at $0.013$; the utilitarian and max-min aggregations of the same game values give up more
than twenty times as much. On the \texttt{Qwen3-8B} backbone \NBPO{} has the highest score in three
of the four pairwise columns and on TruthfulQA, and the three reward-model baselines land at or
below the reference on Arena-Hard and below it on AlpacaEval. These prompts come from Arena-Hard, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench,
IFEval and TruthfulQA and are external to every training set in this paper, so this block is the
one place the \texttt{Qwen3-8B} panel can be read without the caveat of
Appendix~\ref{sec:prompt-audit}.

\paragraph{Separating representation and aggregation.}
Table~\ref{tab:multi-dataset-external} compares each method at its own selected configuration, so a
gap there can come from the aggregation rule or from how far that rule happens to move the policy.
The rules do not move it equally: on the frozen pools the weight vectors they induce differ in
$\ell_1$ norm by factors of $14$ to $55$, because the Nash dual carries raw $\lambda_k=1/s_k$ with
$\|\lambda\|_1=\sum_k 1/s_k$ while the utilitarian rule sits at $\lambda=\mathbf{1}$ and the max-min
rules on the simplex. Table~\ref{tab:component-ablation} therefore rebuilds the ablation under a
matched design. On each panel the five variants share the response pool, the dual solution's
$\eta_t$, the opponent fixed point, the target estimator of \eqref{eq:binary-target-mean}, the
optimiser budget, and the total $\ell_1$ weight norm --- the controls are rescaled to the Nash
arm's, since Nash's own magnitude is fixed by the dual --- so they differ only in the direction of
the aggregation weights or in whether the objective value is the adaptive game or a fixed reference.
Every entry sits closer to the reference than its counterpart in
Table~\ref{tab:multi-dataset-external} because these fits use the $\eta_t$ that the pooled-entropy
rule selects and a smaller learning rate, not because of the weight normalisation; levels are
therefore not comparable across the two tables, and the comparison within a column is what this
table is for.

Two readings change. Nash aggregation survives matching in direction but not in magnitude: it has
the highest matched worst-objective rate on \textsc{UltraFeedback} and is within $0.006$ of the
best on the other two panels, and it is never behind any rule by more than the paired interval. Its
advantage over the two max-min rules is resolved only on \textsc{SafeRLHF}, at $+0.023\,[0.012,
0.034]$ over surplus max-min and $+0.026\,[0.015, 0.038]$ over absolute max-min; against the
utilitarian rule the paired differences are $+0.006$, $+0.005$ and $+0.000$, all straddling zero.
The much larger unmatched gaps therefore reflect step size, not the bargaining weights. The
representation comparison changes sign and loses significance: with the step matched, the adaptive
game and the fixed reference are indistinguishable on all three panels, at $+0.005\,[-0.007,
0.016]$, $-0.006\,[-0.018, 0.014]$ and $-0.001\,[-0.011, 0.008]$ on the worst objective. We
previously read an unmatched $0.036$ advantage for the fixed reference on \textsc{UltraFeedback} as
evidence against the adaptive opponent; that reading does not survive the matched design, and we
report the honest conclusion that this experiment does not separate the two representations.
Appendix~\ref{app:representation-diagnostics} isolates where the finite-pool realisation departs
from the population target.

