\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
\paragraph{General pairwise preferences.}
NLHF and its scalable variants optimize directly against general pairwise preference models rather than fitting scalar rewards \citep{munos2024nash,wu2025sppo,zhang2025iterative,calandriello2024online,rosset2024direct,zhou2025egpo}. Blackwell-style methods extend this view to vector-valued feedback and target sets \citep{blackwell1956analog,bhatia2020multicriteria}. PROSPER introduces a maximum-entropy Blackwell winner with a scalable single-policy update and an inner worst-case choice over objective weights and opponents \citep{zhang2026prosper}. \NBPO{} instead keeps one regularized game value per objective and applies Nash bargaining across the improvements over a shared fallback.

\paragraph{Reward-based multi-objective alignment.}
MODPO, Rewarded Soups, and Panacea expose user-specified trade-offs through scalarization, policy interpolation, or preference conditioning \citep{zhou2024beyond,rame2023rewarded,zhong2024panacea}. MaxMin-RLHF and RMOD optimize weakest-objective criteria, while MOPO designates a primary objective and lower-bound constraints for the others \citep{chakraborty2024maxmin,son2026rmod,agnihotri2026mopo}. These methods are important empirical baselines because they explicitly fit or use scalar objective rewards, whereas \NBPO{} learns from general pairwise feedback without assuming that each objective is transitive.

\paragraph{Social choice and bargaining.}
The Nash solution and its Pareto, uniqueness, and affine-invariance properties are classical \citep{nash1950}. Social-choice work argues that alignment should state its aggregation rule explicitly \citep{conitzer2024social}. Multi-party RLHF studies utilitarian, Nash, and leximin welfare over party-specific learned rewards and separately considers reward-free von Neumann solutions \citep{zhong2024nashwelfare}; Nash bargaining has also been used to combine task gradients in multi-task learning \citep{navon2022nashmtl}. Our contribution is not a new bargaining axiom, but a formulation and practical optimizer for applying the classical Nash rule to policy-dependent objective-wise preference-game values.

\section{Conclusion and Limitations}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\NBPO{} separates two decisions in multi-objective preference alignment: representing possibly cyclic comparisons within each objective and selecting one compromise across objectives. It uses regularized preference games for the first decision and the classical Nash bargaining rule for the second. The bargaining guarantees are inherited once the policy-induced game-value set satisfies the standard conditions; our main contribution is the constrained and partition-free optimization procedure that makes this construction trainable from binary comparisons. The exact population proximal iteration achieves an $O(1/T)$ last-iterate Nash-welfare gap.

Several limitations remain, and the completed comparison sharpens them. The matched component
ablation does not establish an empirical benefit from adaptive game values: once the proximal step
is matched, the adaptive game and the fixed reference are statistically indistinguishable on all
three panels, so this experiment neither credits nor debits the adaptive opponent. The matched
ranking among aggregation rules is also weaker than the unmatched one: Nash has the best or
near-best matched worst-objective rate on every panel and is never behind by more than the paired
interval, but its advantage over the max-min rules is resolved only on \textsc{SafeRLHF} and its
advantage over the utilitarian rule is not resolved anywhere. The cross-method comparison likewise
does not show \NBPO{} attaining the highest worst-objective win rate: Panacea leads it on two
panels and the game-aggregation controls lead it on \textsc{SafeRLHF}. What the measurements do
support is the weaker property the bargaining axioms actually assert: \NBPO{} stays above the
reference on every objective of every panel, and it is the only method that does so without either
fitting a scalar reward per objective or being handed a floor to respect --- the three others that
manage it (MOPO, Rewarded Soups, Panacea) do one or the other by construction. The neural algorithm
also approximates the policy--opponent fixed point, dual solution, and regression target from
finite samples, whereas the convergence theorem assumes exact population updates. The
joint-improvement assumption can fail when no policy improves every objective over the chosen
reference; in that case a positive-surplus Nash solution does not exist. Improvements in game value
should not be interpreted as per-prompt safety guarantees.

\subsection*{AI use statement}
In this work, generative AI tools were used to critique the problem formulation, assist with mathematical derivations and proof presentation, propose experimental checks, and edit manuscript text. The authors are responsible for independently verifying every theorem, citation, implementation, empirical result, and disclosure in the final submission.

\subsection*{Ethics statement}
This work studies aggregation rules for language-model alignment, including harmlessness as one objective. Improvement in a game value is not an absolute safety certificate. Safety-critical deployment should retain hard constraints and independent safety evaluations in addition to the bargaining objective.

\subsection*{Reproducibility statement}
Algorithm~\ref{alg:nbpo} specifies the constrained training procedure. Appendix~\ref{app:proofs} provides the proximal-dual derivation and population convergence proof; Appendices~\ref{app:hyperparameters}, \ref{app:optimization-diagnostics} and~\ref{app:labeling} give the experimental protocol for both configurations, the optimization and regression diagnostics, and the judge details; Appendix~\ref{sec:prompt-audit} gives the evaluation-prompt contamination audit and the rebuild it prompted. Every policy behind a number in Tables~\ref{tab:general-benchmark-completion}, \ref{tab:multi-dataset-external} and~\ref{tab:component-ablation} is released as a public checkpoint, together with the frozen response pools, the dual solver artifacts (weights, surpluses and fixed-point residuals), the pair files with their target columns, and the judge prompts and per-prompt verdicts behind every win rate; the repository is withheld here only to preserve anonymity and is included in the submission package. Seeds, exact sampling budgets, and the prompt-hash lists used for the disjointness audit are part of that release.

