\section{Implementation and Hyperparameters}
\label{app:hyperparameters}
Following prior preference-optimization and multi-objective alignment work, we report response generation, preference scoring, policy optimization, method-specific loop parameters, and compute separately \citep{munos2024nash,zhang2025iterative,zhong2024panacea,zhang2026prosper}. Tables~\ref{tab:data-sampling-hparams} and~\ref{tab:policy-training-hparams} record both configurations that appear in the paper, because they differ. The arms of the primary comparison sit at $\eta_t=1$ with one opponent reweighting per dual update. That is not the value the pooled-entropy rule of Table~\ref{tab:eta-selection} selects, and the discrepancy is why we measured both: on every panel, at both $\eta_t$ values, we recorded whether the fitted policy beats $\pi=\pi_t$ on a prompt-disjoint held-out split of its own pair set. Table~\ref{tab:regression-realization} reports the result, and it selects $\eta_t=1$ on all three panels by a wide margin. The matched-ablation arms deliberately use the pooled-entropy $\eta_t$, the iterated fixed point, and a correspondingly smaller learning rate, which is a factor of $15$ to $20$ smaller in the proximal target the regression is asked to realize; that is why their levels are not comparable to the primary table's. A cell is left empty only where the corresponding quantity does not apply.

