\section{Evaluation-Prompt Contamination Audit}
\label{sec:prompt-audit}
Every arm's training pairs were hashed against its own panel's evaluation prompts, rather than
relying on the split having been taken correctly. Table~\ref{tab:prompt-audit} reports the result.
Two panels and most arms are clean, and two failures are worth stating plainly.

On \textsc{UltraFeedback} the released \NBPO{} arm was fitted on pairs whose prompts include $629$
of the panel's $698$ evaluation prompts. The cause is bookkeeping rather than method: this panel's
response pool was regenerated for the present campaign and its prompts were drawn independently of
the earlier campaign from which the evaluation list was inherited, so the two collided. The released
stage runner refuses to start when the training, monitoring and evaluation prompt sets are not
pairwise disjoint, and this arm was built by calling the dual solver and pair builder directly
rather than through it, which is how the collision went unnoticed; the rebuild is checked against
the same condition explicitly. The
reward-model baselines escaped it because their pair builder takes the evaluation pool as an
explicit exclusion list, and the fixed-reference and adaptive-game arms of the earlier campaign
escaped it because they predate the regenerated pool. The consequence is that the released arm was
the only \textsc{UltraFeedback} entry measured partly in sample, and the bias runs in its own
favour, so the panel's conclusion --- that \NBPO{} does not lead the worst-objective rate there ---
is conservative under it. We nonetheless rebuilt the arm on the identical configuration with the
$629$ offending prompts and their pairs removed ($37{,}800\to34{,}026$ training rows over $5{,}671$
prompts), and Table~\ref{tab:multi-dataset-external} reports the rebuilt arm. The rebuild lands on
worst-objective $0.524$ and average $0.535$, against $0.524$ and $0.540$ for the arm it replaces:
the defect was real and had to be repaired, but on this panel it was not what produced the number.
The rebuilt arm also fits its own target better, which is the reason to prefer it beyond
cleanliness --- held-out normalized MSE $0.983$ against $0.998$, and held-out $R^2$ $+0.017$ against
$+0.002$ (Table~\ref{tab:regression-realization}).

The matched ablation arms inherit a much smaller share of the same collision, $51$ of $698$, because
they are fitted on a subsample of that pool. All five of them share one pair set by construction, so
whatever bias those $51$ prompts introduce is common to every column of
Table~\ref{tab:component-ablation} and cancels in the paired differences that table reports; the
comparison it is built for is between arms on the same prompts, not between an arm and the
reference.

On the \textsc{UltraFeedback}/Qwen panel the same collision is total: the $300$-prompt response
pool contains all $100$ evaluation prompts, and $99$ of them survive into every arm's training
pairs. That panel is therefore reported only through the
general-capability benchmarks of Table~\ref{tab:general-benchmark-completion}, whose prompts come
from Arena-Hard, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench, IFEval and TruthfulQA and are external to every training
set here. We do not report an objective-wise win rate for the Qwen backbone, and the reader should
treat it as a backbone-transfer check on general capability rather than as a fourth panel of the
primary comparison.

