\section{Diagnosing the Adaptive-Game Gap}
\label{app:representation-diagnostics}
An earlier version of this paper reported that the simpler fixed-reference Nash variant had the
better point estimate, and asked which of three explanations was responsible: the adaptive
population objective may not help on this pool, the neural regression may fail to realize its
target, or the pool may contain too little disagreement between objectives for an adaptive opponent
to matter. All three now have measurements. The first thing they show is that the comparison itself
was confounded, and that once it is unconfounded the advantage disappears in both directions: the
matched rows of Table~\ref{tab:component-ablation} put the two representations within $0.006$ of
each other on all three panels, with every paired interval straddling zero.

\paragraph{The comparison was not matched.} Fixed reference is the same solver at
$\beta\to\infty$, which collapses the opponent onto $\piref$ and makes the opponent fixed point
of \eqref{eq:fixed-opponent-update} hold by construction: its solver artifact records a fixed-point
residual of exactly $0$. The adaptive arm it was compared against was solved with a single
policy--opponent step, whose measured residual was $0.9999$. The published contrast was therefore
between a converged arm and an unconverged one, and attributed the difference to the representation.
The two surpluses are not comparable either: each is measured against its own opponent, and a
weaker opponent yields a larger surplus by definition. Every arm in
Table~\ref{tab:component-ablation} is now solved to the same fixed point
(Table~\ref{tab:matched-welfare}) and takes the same total $\ell_1$ proximal step, and the decisive
comparison is the external win rate $W^{\rm eval}$, which does not depend on either side's
opponent.

\paragraph{The regression did fail to realize its target, and the fix was the data.}
Table~\ref{tab:regression-realization} measures the fit directly. The released
\textsc{UltraFeedback} policy's log-ratio change was uncorrelated with the target it was fitted to:
normalized MSE $1.0005$ on its own training pairs, $R^2=-0.0005$, and a training loss flat from step
$10$ to step $300$. A policy that does not realize its target is not an approximation of the
\NBPO{} update at all, which is the first thing that had to be fixed before any comparison between
weight rules could mean anything.

Refitting the same configuration on the contamination-free pair set of
Appendix~\ref{sec:prompt-audit} changes that, and so does simply measuring the other two panels,
which had never been diagnosed at their released $\eta_t$. At the selected step size every panel now
beats the zero predictor on prompt-disjoint held-out pairs: normalized MSE $0.983$, $0.902$ and
$0.959$, with held-out $R^2$ of $+0.017$, $+0.098$ and $+0.041$ and held-out correlations between
$0.14$ and $0.33$. The finite-pool target is therefore realizable and does generalize, modestly, at
this data scale --- the earlier reading that it does not was drawn from one contaminated fit and
from rebuilds at a much smaller $\eta_t$. Those small-$\eta_t$ rebuilds are in the table too, and
they are the failure case: at the $\eta_t$ the pooled-entropy rule selects, the same optimizer
overshoots a target fifteen to twenty times smaller and lands at held-out normalized MSE $2.65$ and
$3.50$. This is why the primary comparison selects $\eta_t$ by the held-out realization criterion
rather than by pooled entropy; the two rules disagree, both are computed without touching evaluation
data, and only one of them is about whether the policy realizes the update. A control that
precomputes $\mu$ twice and differences the two passes gives $\|h\|_{\rm rms}=0$ exactly, so none
of these numbers is measurement noise.

\paragraph{The $\eta_t$ rule was set on training data and the evaluation agrees with it.}
Selecting $\eta_t$ by the held-out realization criterion rather than by pooled entropy is a decision
made entirely on the training side, and it is worth reporting what the metric it never saw says
about it. We retrained the \textsc{TL;DR} and \textsc{SafeRLHF} arms at the $\eta_t$ the
pooled-entropy rule selects, changing nothing else, and judged them with the same independent
evaluator. On \textsc{TL;DR} the pooled-entropy arm reaches worst-objective $0.494$ against $0.528$
for the selected arm, and it is the one setting of \NBPO{} we have measured that falls \emph{below}
the reference on one of its objectives; on \textsc{SafeRLHF} it reaches $0.617$ against $0.696$.
The realization criterion and the external win rate therefore rank the two $\eta_t$ values the same
way on both panels, with a gap of $0.034$ and $0.079$. This is not a validation of the criterion in
any strong sense --- two panels and two $\eta_t$ values are not a study of selection rules --- but it
does mean the choice reported in Table~\ref{tab:multi-dataset-external} is not one the evaluation
data was used to make, and is not one the evaluation data contradicts.

\paragraph{The pool is not short of disagreement.}
Table~\ref{tab:objective-disagreement} rules out the third explanation. On the frozen pools no
response is top under every objective for $46\%$ of \textsc{UltraFeedback} prompts, $69\%$ of
\textsc{TL;DR} prompts and $23\%$ of \textsc{SafeRLHF} prompts, counting ties as agreement, and the
objective-wise orderings disagree on $0.24$ to $0.34$ of response pairs. Splitting
\textsc{UltraFeedback} at the median disagreement (Table~\ref{tab:cycle-conditioned}) shows that
bargaining is genuinely harder where the objectives conflict --- the minimum surplus falls from
$0.081$ to $0.046$ --- but that the gap between the adaptive and fixed-reference representations
does not widen there.

